  builds it.

  [1mTesting[0m

  [38;5;153mnode --test[39m added as [38;5;153mnpm test[39m, zero dependencies. The tests that matter:

  - [1mThe canary:[0m pass the entire [38;5;153mformData[39m object — password included — into [38;5;153mlog.info[39m, serialize the record, assert the
    password [3mvalue[0m and the key [38;5;153mpassword[39m both appear nowhere. This is the test that has to keep passing forever.
  - Allowlist drops unknown keys and reports [38;5;153mdroppedFields[39m.
  - Allowlisting [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, or [38;5;153msecret[39m throws, so the deny list can't be quietly widened.
  - Level threshold filters correctly.
  - Buffer behavior against a fake sink and injected clock: flushes at the size threshold, flushes at the time
    threshold, re-queues on failure, drops oldest at cap, breaker opens after 3 failures and closes after the cooldown.
  - Node adapter emits one parseable JSON object per line.

  The DOM-dependent parts of [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and the real [38;5;153msendBeacon[39m path are [1mnot[0m covered — that needs the e2e tooling you
  declined. Worth naming: the wiring between the form and the logger will be verified by reading, not by tests.

  [1mTwo open items I need before implementation[0m

  1. [1mThe collector URL[0m — or confirmation that someone is building the endpoint. The design keeps the sink pluggable so
     this doesn't block writing the code, but shipping it without a live collector means the browser side logs nothing
     useful.
  2. [1mRetention[0m — not my call, but whoever owns the collector should set one. Session ids plus IP addresses in access
     logs can re-identify users regardless of how careful the record schema is.
